<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Qualitative Safety Analysis</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We visualize a three representative method’s contrast, risk-aware expressive generator RADAC, risk-neutral expressive generator DiffusionQL, and anchor-perturb risk-averse method ORAAC. Fig. <a href="#S5.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 5.2 Qualitative Safety Analysis ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ RAMAC: Multimodal Risk-Aware Offline Reinforcement Learning and the Role of Behavior Regularization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> plots the monitored distribution of policies against safe regions. RADAC concentrate probability mass inside or near safe boundary while <em id="S5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">actively reallocates probability onto high‑return modes that lie within the safety regions</em>. DiffusionQL is tightly concentrated around zero because rare, high penalties depress bootstrapped values near the safe boundary. On the other hand, ORAAC regularizes toward a behavior anchor and thus settles at a low density between‑mode when the anchor lies in risky-region. These features can be further observed in Table <a href="#A4.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ Pareto Frontier Analysis: Return vs. Safety Violations ‣ D.2 Extended Stochastic-D4RL Results ‣ Appendix D Additional Experimental Results ‣ RAMAC: Multimodal Risk-Aware Offline Reinforcement Learning and the Role of Behavior Regularization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> in App. <a href="#A4.SS2.SSS0.Px3" title="Pareto Frontier Analysis: Return vs. Safety Violations ‣ D.2 Extended Stochastic-D4RL Results ‣ Appendix D Additional Experimental Results ‣ RAMAC: Multimodal Risk-Aware Offline Reinforcement Learning and the Role of Behavior Regularization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.2</span></a></p>
</div>
